<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moctezuma I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1398–<a href="/years/1469">1469</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Motecuhzoma Ilhuicamina, Montezuma I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moctezuma I, also called Motecuhzoma Ilhuicamina, was the fifth ruler (tlatoani) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, reigning from 1440 to 1469. With his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a> he expanded the Aztec <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a> far beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, conquering Chalco, Coixtlahuaca and lands towards the Gulf coast. His reign also saw a great famine in the 1450s and the building of a dike across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a> designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i.jpg" alt="Moctezuma I" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huehuemoteuczoma_mendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Moctezuma was the son of Huitzilihuitl and Queen Miahuaxihuitl, grandson of the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpopoca">Chimalpopoca</a> and Tlacaelel I. After Chimalpopoca was assassinated, Moctezuma and Tlacaelel led a militant group of young nobles who chose their half-uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> as ruler, and under Itzcoatl the two brothers served as generals. The same group elected Moctezuma in 1440, at the age of 42, after Itzcoatl died. He took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huey-tlatoani">Great Speaker</a> because he spoke for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and for the peoples under their control, and the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> crowned him with a turquoise diadem known as the fire crown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Moctezuma strengthened the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>. Under its terms two fifths of the spoils went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, two fifths to Texcoco and one fifth to Tlacopan. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> of Texcoco he organised the building of a double aqueduct that brought fresh water to Tenochtitlan. His armies took the Aztec domain beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> to the Gulf Coast, subjugating the Huastec and Totonac peoples. About 1458 he led an expedition against the Mixtec city-state of Coixtlahuaca and defeated it despite help from Tlaxcala and Huexotzinco. Most of the defeated princes kept their positions, but the Mixtec ruler Atonal was ritually strangled and his family enslaved.</p><p>Natural disasters struck. Locusts destroyed crops in 1446 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a> flooded the city in 1449. Frost and drought began in 1450 and lasted four years, and in the famine some sold their children or themselves into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Believing the gods angry, Moctezuma supported more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a>, which led to the Flower War against Huejotzingo, Tlaxcala and Cholula. He also issued rules fixing what each class could wear and how its houses could look, and kept the power of the death sentence for himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Moctezuma I was an ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>, who met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>. He died in 1469. Several Southwestern Native American peoples, notably the Tohono O'odham and the Pueblo, tell of a hero-god Moctezuma whose legend differs from the historical ruler but probably grew from tales of him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moctezuma I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later writers, above all the mestizo historian Diego Muñoz Camargo, describe a trade embargo begun under <b>Moctezuma I</b> in the mid-fifteenth century on salt, cotton, tropical feathers, cacao, gold and silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Moctezuma I</b> (r. 1440–1469) created the title quauhpilli, a nobility for life won by outstanding service; Moctezuma II (r. 1502–1520) made such promotion harder and barred commoners and the illegitimate sons of nobles from his palace and from high office.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>After a war lasting from the 1440s, the Aztec Empire under <b>Moctezuma I</b> conquered Chalco in 1465 and made it a tributary.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalco">Chalco</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1458 an army of the Aztec Empire under <b>Moctezuma I</b> conquered Coixtlahuaca and executed Atonal, and the region became a tribute province.</blockquote><a href="/state/coixtlahuaca">Coixtlahuaca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1444" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1872 1444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Moctezuma I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1444">What was here in 1444? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
